{"context":"The Runtime is mature enough to serve as an Engineering Knowledge Runtime for AI agents: the **Runtime API** and the **Authoring API** (ADR-014) are the one sanctioned boundary; retrieval is served by `eka get` (ADR-015 — canonical CKO JSON); understanding is served by `eka context` (ADR-021 — the deterministic Engineering Context Object, whose stated purpose is the consumer class the Runtime was shaped for: AI agents, MCP, Atrium); authoring is served by the draft-publish workflow (`eka new` / `eka edit` / `eka publish` / `eka discard`, ADR-016 + spec-standard-v2 §8) and the transition/note commands (ADR-019). ADR-021 Decision 9 drew the **AI boundary**: *Context Object → future AI adapter → LLM, model-agnostic* — the deterministic object is the contract; adapters live on the far side.\n\nWhat does not yet exist is the **behavior layer**: what an AI agent should *do* with these capabilities — the mental model, the workflows, the retrieval/authoring/modification/review discipline, and the safety boundaries. Without it, an agent with CLI access to a Runtime-enabled project has no canonical instruction on how to be EKA-aware: it may scan every representation instead of using `eka context`, may mutate knowledge instead of revising it, may bypass validation, or may treat derived output as canonical.\n\nA future **MCP integration** is expected — MCP would expose Runtime capabilities as a wire transport — but MCP is intentionally outside this milestone: it introduces a significantly larger protocol and tooling surface, and the behavior layer must exist and be proven before the transport matters. The Runtime already reserves the slot: ADR-014 §Future Extensibility names MCP as a thin adapter over the same services; ADR-011 §Decision 8 reserved the wire layer; the machine interface (ADR-015) and the Context Object (ADR-021) are the contracts an MCP server would wrap.\n\nThis milestone implements the behavior layer: the **EKA AI Skill Pack** — modular skill definitions teaching agents what EKA is, how to reason about Engineering Domains, how to retrieve canonical knowledge, construct context, author and modify knowledge safely, review it, and preserve EKA invariants — running on the existing CLI, with MCP explicitly deferred.","decision":"1. **The Skill Pack is a behavior layer, not a tool layer.** Skills define how an AI agent should behave (mental model, workflows, safety rules); the **CLI is the current execution interface**; the Runtime is the knowledge store. The pack adds **zero** runtime surface: no new Runtime API, no new CLI commands, no duplicated Runtime logic — every command the skills teach already exists (inspection rule: agents verify against `eka help`; the pack only references existing commands).\n\n2. **The pack is a zone of this repository (`skills/`) with modular skills, not one monolithic instruction file.** One skill per concern, each a `SKILL.md` (name + description frontmatter, markdown body — the widely adopted convention): `eka-router` (the routing decision tree — pick the matching skill, stop after routing), `eka-orientation` (the mental model), `eka-project-understanding` (context-first project context), `eka-knowledge-retrieval` (`eka get` vs `eka context` vs `eka view`), `eka-knowledge-authoring` (draft → validate → publish), `eka-knowledge-modification` (immutable revisions, transitions), `eka-knowledge-review` (EKA-native validation), `eka-adoption` (bringing non-EKA projects into EKA — assessment, legacy-doc classification, state inference with honesty guardrails, the docs-tree and workspace-draft migration paths, greenfield capture), `eka-engineering-workflow` (the canonical-domain spine, methodology-independent), `eka-troubleshooting` (refusal classes and deterministic fixes). Skill names carry the `eka-` prefix to avoid collisions with external skill packs. **Each skill directory is self-contained and portable** (the convention of the skill ecosystems: a skill = one folder, copied whole into an agent's skill configuration, e.g. `~/.config/opencode/skills/\u003cname\u003e/` or `.opencode/skills/\u003cname\u003e/`); skill resources therefore live **inside** the skill folder — the draft templates per token family sit in `eka-knowledge-authoring/templates/` (derived from the actual `eka new`/`eka note` scaffolds), referenced by relative paths from `SKILL.md`. Pack-level material that agents do not consume at runtime stays outside skill folders: `scripts/smoke-test.sh` (the executable pack validation over the Reference Project), `manifest.yaml` (pack version, `requiresEka` CLI contract, skill index), `commands/` (user-invoked agent workflows packaged for opencode with provider-agnostic bodies — `eka-discuss`, the interactive planning discussion through idea → discuss → propose → validate → approved → publish that creates knowledge drafts and delegates review to sub-agents without touching source code; and `eka-execute`, the autonomous execution of approved planning with the EKA state machine, team review, per-item checkpoints, a resume protocol whose core principle is *state on disk, not in context* — deterministic `eka get`/`eka context` make resume a re-derivation, never a memory — and a branch/worktree strategy: every item is implemented in its own branch + worktree created from the latest development branch snapshot and merged back to the development branch (PR/MR or direct merge) before the `done` transition, leveraging ADR-017's identity-based resolution so EKA commands work inside worktrees), and `docs/` (installation \u0026 discovery, the AI workflow, the MCP boundary, the Reference Project validation report).\n\n3. **Provider- and ecosystem-agnostic.** The skill content is the deliverable; the `SKILL.md` envelope is a convention, not a vendor lock. Any agent ecosystem (Claude Skills, opencode, custom prompt packs) consumes the content directly or wrapped. No agent-runtime-specific code, no protocol, no tool definitions.\n\n4. **Context-first is the operational core.** The pack's primary mental model is `Understand → Context → Reason → Change → Validate → Publish`: agents prefer obtaining project context before modifying knowledge or source code when the task depends on intent, architecture, planning, or constraints — via `eka sync` (precondition), `eka get` (the knowledge map), and `eka context --depth engineering` (the constraints in force). Blind modification based solely on source code is discouraged explicitly.\n\n5. **Explicit retrieval distinction.** The pack keeps `eka get` (retrieve — canonical machine-readable CKO JSON), `eka context` (understand — the deterministic Context Object around one subject), and `eka view` (visualize — human projections) formally distinct, mirroring ADR-015/ADR-021: agents pick by question type, never conflate them, never parse Markdown for runtime answers, and never treat projections/contexts/summaries as canonical.\n\n6. **Immutability is enforced behaviorally.** The pack teaches the append-only discipline of ADR-011: never mutate canonical objects; changes are new revisions (new drafts published as the next instance version, `eka transition` for sanctioned state changes with the R13 gates of ADR-019); identity, relationships, state, stratum, traceability, and integrity are preserved; supersession/amendment stays same-stratum (R12, ADR-008).\n\n7. **Authoring goes through the Authoring API surface only.** Agents author via the draft-publish workflow (workspace drafts, `eka new` / `eka publish`) or the legacy docs-tree adapter (`eka validate` + `eka sync`) — never by writing to the canonical store, `eka.db`, or snapshot files. Validation (Draft → Validate → Publish) is mandatory; there is no sanctioned bypass. (The API-only `PublishInline` path exists for future agents/interfaces; the pack does not expose anything beyond the CLI.)\n\n8. **AI safety boundaries are canonical and pack-wide.** The pack establishes hard behavioral constraints: never modify Runtime storage directly, never write SQLite, never bypass the Authoring API or validation, never silently change higher-stratum knowledge to justify lower-stratum implementation, never invent Engineering Knowledge, never overwrite immutable knowledge, never treat generated summaries as canonical. AI-generated knowledge is subject to the normal validation and lifecycle rules (P16: enforcement mechanisms vary, the invariants do not).\n\n9. **The MCP boundary is documented, not implemented.** The pack documents the intended evolution (today: Agent → Skills → CLI → Runtime; future: Agent → Skills → MCP → Runtime) and the separation contract: skills define behavior, MCP will expose Runtime capabilities (the same Runtime API / Authoring API / Context Object contracts), skills are not a substitute for MCP and MCP is not a substitute for skills, skills must not duplicate Runtime logic, and the behavior layer must survive the transport change unmodified. No MCP implementation, no new Runtime API, no new CLI commands in this milestone.\n\n10. **Validation against the Reference Project is part of the deliverable.** The pack's claims are demonstrated, not asserted: the validation report (`skills/docs/reference-project-validation.md`) records executed scenarios against `reference/project` (Feather) in a scratch workspace — sync, domain maps, CKO retrieval, context construction (including the engineering-depth constraint closure), the full authoring loop (scaffold → publish v1 → revise → publish v2 → transition → note), and validation/integrity gates — all green, all through existing commands.","consequences":"- **Positive.** Agents working in EKA-enabled projects get one canonical behavior contract: EKA-aware by instruction, not by luck. The pack is testable (the validation report is reproducible with the documented scratch-workspace commands). The CLI remains the single execution surface — no behavior drifts from Runtime semantics. The Context Object (ADR-021) gains its documented consumer; the machine interface (ADR-015) gains its documented user. Future MCP integration starts with the behavior layer already defined: the skills map onto MCP tools with the same semantics.\n- **Negative.** The pack is instruction-only: it cannot *enforce* the safety boundaries — an agent that ignores them is not blocked by the pack (enforcement lives in the CLI gates and the validator; P16). The pack references CLI behavior; CLI evolution requires pack maintenance (mitigated by the inspection rule, Decision 1, and the pack living in the same repository). Skill files are documentation — they are not scanned by `eka validate` (the zone carries no knowledge tree under the repo root; ADR-018 docs-scoped conformance covers only `\u003croot\u003e/docs`).\n- **Risks.** Agents may treat the pack as an excuse to skip source-code analysis (explicitly rejected: source code and Engineering Knowledge are complementary, neither replaces the other). Agents may over-load skills (mitigated by the discovery table: load only the skill the task matches). The `SKILL.md` convention may differ per ecosystem (mitigated by provider-agnostic content; wrapping changes nothing behavioral).\n\n### Future Extensibility (not implemented)\n\n- **MCP / AI adapter milestone** — the wire layer over the existing Runtime contracts; the skills sit unchanged on top (mapping: retrieval tools = `eka get`/`eka context` surfaces; authoring tools = draft lifecycle; the Context Object is the deterministic prompt-ready contract, ADR-021 Decision 9).\n- **Agent ecosystem packs** — provider-specific wrappers (e.g. a Claude Skills manifest, an opencode skill set) generated from the same content.\n- **`PublishInline` exposure** — the implemented API-only single-prompt authoring path may gain a CLI/agent command in a future milestone, entering through the same Authoring API and validation.\n- **Skill pack versioning** — explicit pack version + compatibility matrix with CLI versions, if the pack outgrows \"versioned with the repository\".\n\n### References\n\n- [ADR-014](adr-014-runtime-interface-architecture.md) — the Runtime Kernel and the Authoring API: the boundary the pack's behavior layer sits on; §Future Extensibility reserves MCP/Atrium consumers\n- [ADR-015](adr-015-machine-retrieval-interface.md) — `eka get`, the machine interface the retrieval skill teaches\n- [ADR-021](adr-021-context-engine.md) — the Context Engine and the AI boundary; the context-first workflow the pack operationalizes\n- [ADR-011](adr-011-immutable-engineering-knowledge-model.md) — the immutable model the modification skill enforces behaviorally\n- [ADR-008](adr-008-engineering-domain-model.md) — Engineering Domains and the Stratum Authority Invariant the orientation/review skills teach\n- [ADR-016](adr-016-standard-v2-json-authoring.md) — JSON-native authoring and the draft workflow the authoring skill teaches\n- [ADR-019](adr-019-work-item-transitions-notes-gates.md) — transitions, notes, and the R13 gates the modification/workflow skills teach\n- [ADR-018](adr-018-repository-context.md) — the `eka.yaml` repository gate every repo-scoped command enforces\n- [ADR-010](adr-010-synchronization-model.md) — `eka sync`, the precondition of every runtime read the skills use\n- The Skill Pack — [`../../skills/README.md`](../../skills/README.md) (index, discovery, safety boundaries), the ten skills under [`../../skills/`](../../skills/), [`../../skills/manifest.yaml`](../../skills/manifest.yaml) (pack version + `requiresEka` contract), [`../../skills/commands/README.md`](../../skills/commands/README.md) (the user-invoked commands `eka-discuss`/`eka-execute`, provider mapping, resume architecture), [`../../skills/eka-knowledge-authoring/templates/README.md`](../../skills/eka-knowledge-authoring/templates/README.md) (reference drafts per token family), [`../../skills/docs/workflow.md`](../../skills/docs/workflow.md) (the AI workflow), [`../../skills/docs/mcp-boundary.md`](../../skills/docs/mcp-boundary.md) (the future MCP integration boundary), [`../../skills/docs/reference-project-validation.md`](../../skills/docs/reference-project-validation.md) (validation evidence against the Reference Project), [`../../skills/scripts/smoke-test.sh`](../../skills/scripts/smoke-test.sh) (the executable pack validation)\n- ADR index: [`../adr-summary.md`](../adr-summary.md)","alternativesConsidered":"- **One monolithic instruction file** — rejected: forces every task to load everything; contradicts modular discovery; harder to maintain per concern.\n- **Implement MCP in this milestone** — rejected: a larger protocol/tooling surface; the behavior layer must exist and be proven first; ADR-014/ADR-011 already reserve the wire slot.\n- **Extend the CLI with AI-specific commands** — rejected: the existing surface (get/context/validate/integrity/new/publish/transition/note/sync) is sufficient; AI-specific commands would duplicate Runtime semantics (Decision 1).\n- **Skills as the future transport (skills replacing MCP)** — rejected: skills define behavior, MCP transports capabilities; conflating them couples behavior to protocol (Decision 9).\n- **Validate skills with an agent runtime in the test suite** — rejected for v0.1: runtime-dependent and non-deterministic; the Reference Project validation report is the deterministic evidence (Decision 10)."}